<details class="methodology">
  <summary>Methodology</summary>
  <h4>1. Data</h4>
  <p>Official Elections BC results by electoral district (2017, 2020 and 2024, DataBC open data),
  provincial polls published since 2017 (Wikipedia), regional breakdowns of 48 polls taken from the
  pollsters' reports, 34 of them since the 2024 election, and the official 2026 candidate list. The
  2020 results are transposed onto the 2023 map: the election-day votes of each voting area are
  split between the 2023 districts according to the 2024 registered voters in the overlap of voting
  areas, and advance, mail-in and special votes, known only by district, follow for each party the
  distribution of its election-day votes. Boundaries of the 93 districts of the 2023 redistribution
  (Elections BC), simplified for display.</p>
  <h4>2. Provincial voting intentions</h4>
  <p>The poll average is a Gaussian process on the isometric log-ratios of the shares, fitted to the
  polls published since the 2024 election (each firm's last poll per week), with changepoints at
  leadership changes and party-system realignments, and predicted at election day. On the eve of
  the 2020 and 2024 elections, the mean absolute error of this average is 1.2 and 1.0 points. A
  party measured province-wide but running in only some districts has its share scaled to p·c, c
  being the share of the 2024 votes cast in the districts where it has a candidate.</p>
  <h4>3. Districts</h4>
  <p>The 2024 results are carried to the provincial composition by two swing forms, each in half of the
  draws. By transfer, a declining party loses the same proportion of its votes everywhere and the freed
  votes go to the growing parties, in proportion to their gains; by additive swing, each party gains or
  loses the same number of points everywhere. In both, a party with no candidate in a district receives
  no votes there. Evaluated at known provincial shares, the transfer gives a root mean square error of
  district shares of 6.2 points (2017→2020) and 6.5 points (2020→2024), the additive swing 5.7 and 7.4,
  a proportional swing 6.1 and 17.3; the mixture of the first two forms has, on average, the best log-likelihood of
  the winners on the eve of both elections. A regional shift taken from the breakdowns (Metro
  Vancouver, Vancouver Island, rest of the province) and an incumbency effect (open seat, incumbent
  running under another banner), evaluated the same way, do not improve the forecast: their weight is
  zero.</p>
  <h4>4. Uncertainty</h4>
  <p>Each draw combines a draw of the poll average, a provincial shock in centred log-ratios with
  variance τ²/s for a party of share s, τ being estimated on the eve-of-election poll errors of 2020
  and 2024, and, for each district, a local deviation drawn from the errors of the 2017→2020 and
  2020→2024 transitions under the same swing form, centred by region. On the eve of the 2020 and 2024
  elections, each evaluated with parameters estimated without it, all 6 of the 90% intervals of seats
  by party contain the result; the absolute seat error of the median scenario (medoid) is 0 and 6, and
  the winner is correct in 73 of 87 and 79 of 93 districts.</p>
  <h4>5. Government scenarios and certainty</h4>
  <p>Each draw is assigned to a single government scenario: majority when the first party reaches 47
  seats, minority otherwise. For a minority government, the parties whose seats, added to those of
  the first party, reach a majority are listed, excluding the Official Opposition (second party in
  seats): these parties hold the balance of power. In the certainty chart, each riding is assigned to its favourite party and classed by that party's probability of winning: safe (95% or more), likely (75 to 95%), lean (60 to 75%), toss-up (under 60%).</p>
</details>
